event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0231ab5961a06bc4006a5fd5de56448196b38db8f23891d703","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":339,"text":"The latest major news on NASA’s **Mars Perseverance rover** is that it has been studying some of the **oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover** and has also hit a big driving milestone.\n\n- **Ancient impact record found:** NASA/JPL reported on **July 15, 2026** that Perseverance examined a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of layered rock on the rim of Jezero Crater, called the **“Broom Point member.”** Scientists think these rocks were built up by **repeated asteroid impacts** more than **3.9 billion years ago**, offering a rare look at the early solar system and ancient Martian crust【6:2†source】.\n\n- **Perseverance completed a “marathon” on Mars:** Around the same time, NASA announced that the rover had driven **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** across Mars, reaching the marathon-distance mark on its **1,890th Martian day**, or sol. It did this in about **five years and four months**, much faster than Opportunity, which took more than 11 years to cover the same distance【6:0†source】.\n\n- **It is now exploring beyond Jezero Crater:** NASA’s Mars 2020 news page lists recent features showing Perseverance at and around **Broom Point** and **Arbot**, part of its continued exploration west of Jezero Crater’s rim【6:3†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, still driving, and is now investigating extremely ancient rocks that may preserve evidence of Mars’ earliest bombardment history."}

